<http://data.silknow.org/object/ea50688a-f69e-3299-a730-74689b1859f1>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Cylindrical bag of embroidered silk, Great Britain, 1600-1650"@en , "Cylindrical bag of embroidered silk. Hinged at the bottom and embroidered with detached buttonhole stitch in the shape of a bunch of grapes with a vine leaf at the top. With silk thread in shades of red, green, blue and brown. The lining and interior bag are made of green silk. The bag has a drawstring of plaited green silk thread with two embroidered finials in the shape of three grapes."@en , "The Jacobeans loved imitating natural forms in their decorative arts and the shape of this purse resembles a bunch of grapes.  Each grape was worked in the colours of blue, red, green or brown.  A vine leaf has been worked at the top in shades of green. \n \nThe three-dimensional quality of the embroidery is achieved through the use of padding under detached buttonhole stitch.  The threads over each grape are worked independently of the fabric below and attached only around the edges.  \n\nThe delicate embroidery and small size of the bag suggest that it was probably intended to hold a keepsake or to wrap a gift of money, rather than everyday monetary use."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/3d8490c2-e0f5-337b-9612-b688c516c53c> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/0c2905d0-1c73-3064-95b0-5739c67f37da> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/32b65f0a-de17-3495-90f3-1712a9c8a734> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/7d314f1f-f860-3dc0-ba5c-e768d075b9b6> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/2afc08a7-f2ad-3a90-bd42-6590b84836d1> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "The Jacobeans loved imitating natural forms in their decorative arts and the shape of this purse resembles a bunch of grapes.  Each grape was worked in the colours of blue, red, green or brown.  A vine leaf has been worked at the top in shades of green. \n \nThe three-dimensional quality of the embroidery is achieved through the use of padding under detached buttonhole stitch.  The threads over each grape are worked independently of the fabric below and attached only around the edges.  \n\nThe delicate embroidery and small size of the bag suggest that it was probably intended to hold a keepsake or to wrap a gift of money, rather than everyday monetary use."@en , "Cylindrical bag of embroidered silk, Great Britain, 1600-1650"@en , "Cylindrical bag of embroidered silk. Hinged at the bottom and embroidered with detached buttonhole stitch in the shape of a bunch of grapes with a vine leaf at the top. With silk thread in shades of red, green, blue and brown. The lining and interior bag are made of green silk. The bag has a drawstring of plaited green silk thread with two embroidered finials in the shape of three grapes."@en ;
        ecrm:P43_has_dimension         <http://data.silknow.org/object/ea50688a-f69e-3299-a730-74689b1859f1/dimension/4> , <http://data.silknow.org/object/ea50688a-f69e-3299-a730-74689b1859f1/dimension/2> , <http://data.silknow.org/object/ea50688a-f69e-3299-a730-74689b1859f1/dimension/3> , <http://data.silknow.org/object/ea50688a-f69e-3299-a730-74689b1859f1/dimension/1> ;
        dc:identifier                  "T.87-1965" ;
        <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs>
                <https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O158882> .

